# Tablet App 8a — Scaffold, Rep Auth, API Client, Background Location + Ping Posting

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** First half of PLAN.md 8.1/8.2 — a Capacitor+React+TS+Tailwind Android app with rep login, a typed API client, and background location tracking that posts pings to the backend. Second half (offline queue, sync-on-reconnect, check-in/out, task list, goal progress view — PLAN.md 8.3/8.4) is a separate plan, `2026-07-21-tablet-8b-*.md`, mirroring the dashboard's 7a/7b split.

**Verification model, and why it differs from both prior client phases:** `docs/design.md`'s v1 bar is explicit: *"tablet APK buildable"* — not "verified on a device," not "verified in an emulator." Phase 10.3 separately lists *"real-device GPS testing"* as a punch-list item needing customer/user infrastructure, not something this phase attempts. Per-task gates are `npm run typecheck` + `npm run build` (the web layer) + `npx cap sync android && cd android && ./gradlew assembleDebug` (the native layer — genuinely producing a `.apk` file, not just trusting Gradle's exit code). The controller's end-of-plan verification (mirroring the dashboard's real-browser check) is: confirm a real, non-empty `app-debug.apk` exists after the final task. There is no emulator or physical device available in this environment, and per design.md's own scoping that's a legitimate, named-in-advance limitation, not a shortcut — do not attempt to install/boot an Android emulator to "go further" than the stated bar.

**Environment facts, verified empirically before this plan was written (do not skip or "improve" these — they were found the hard way):**
- **JDK 21 is required**, not JDK 17. Capacitor 8's Android Gradle module targets Java source/target compatibility 21; building with JDK 17 fails with `error: invalid source release: 21` at the very first native compile step. `brew install openjdk@21` (not the `openjdk` formula/cask, which installs a different version and needs `sudo` for the cask). Every command in this plan that touches `android/` needs `JAVA_HOME` set to the JDK 21 install.
- **Gradle's default HTTP timeout is too short for this network** — a large (~50MB) dependency (`kotlin-compiler` from Google's Maven repo, pulled in transitively by Capacitor's Android annotation-extraction step) reproducibly truncates mid-download with Gradle's default timeouts (confirmed twice, at two different truncation points — not a one-off blip; confirmed via a direct `curl` of the same URL succeeding cleanly, proving the network path itself is fine). Every `android/gradle.properties` in this project needs these four lines appended (Task 1 does this once; it's inherited by every later task since they all build on the same `android/` project):
  ```
  systemProp.org.gradle.internal.http.connectionTimeout=180000
  systemProp.org.gradle.internal.http.socketTimeout=180000
  systemProp.http.socketTimeout=180000
  systemProp.https.socketTimeout=180000
  ```
- **The full pipeline (Vite scaffold → `cap init` → `cap add android` → `./gradlew assembleDebug`) was verified end-to-end in a throwaway spike and produces a real, installable `.apk`.** `@capacitor-community/background-geolocation@1.2.26`'s own README compatibility table only lists Capacitor up to v7 — this was independently verified NOT to be a real blocker: the plugin's native Android code was spiked against Capacitor 8.4.2 with this exact toolchain and built cleanly (`BUILD SUCCESSFUL`, real `.apk` produced). One informational-only deprecation warning appeared ("incompatible with Gradle 9.0") — harmless today since Capacitor 8's generated project pins Gradle 8.14.3, worth a one-line awareness note for whoever eventually bumps Gradle, not a fix needed now.
- **First-time `cap add android` + Gradle sync is slow** (~7 minutes observed: Gradle wrapper download + first dependency resolution). Subsequent builds are fast (under a minute) once Gradle's cache is warm. Don't assume a hang; a background/long-running Bash call with a generous timeout is the right tool for the first sync specifically.
- Environment variables needed for every Android-touching command in this plan:
  ```bash
  export JAVA_HOME="/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@21/libexec/openjdk.jdk/Contents/Home"
  export ANDROID_HOME="$HOME/Library/Android/sdk"
  export PATH="$JAVA_HOME/bin:$ANDROID_HOME/cmdline-tools/latest/bin:$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools:$PATH"
  ```
  (The Android SDK itself — platform-tools, `platforms;android-34`, `build-tools;34.0.0` — is already installed at `~/Library/Android/sdk` in this environment; no need to reinstall it.)

## Global Constraints

- **Directory**: `tablet/` at the repo root, sibling to `backend/` and `dashboard/`. Same stack as the dashboard otherwise (Vite + React 18 + TypeScript strict + Tailwind CSS v4 via `@tailwindcss/vite`), `npm` as the package manager.
- **Backend contract, verified against the real routers before writing this plan (do not re-derive, use as given):**
  - `POST /api/v1/auth/login` needs `X-Tenant-Id` header (only call that does); `POST /api/v1/auth/refresh` and `GET /api/v1/auth/me` use `Authorization: Bearer` alone. Identical to the dashboard's already-proven contract.
  - `POST /api/v1/pings` — rep-only, body `{recorded_at, latitude, longitude, accuracy?, idempotency_key}`.
  - `POST /api/v1/visits/check-in` — rep-only, body `{idempotency_key, zone_id?, latitude, longitude, notes?, external_reference?}` — **`idempotency_key` is REQUIRED** (added in the `feat/tablet-backend-prereqs` branch specifically so this app can queue-and-retry check-ins safely; do not build this app against an assumption that predates that fix).
  - `POST /api/v1/visits/{id}/check-out` — rep-only, no body.
  - `GET /api/v1/tasks` — rep sees own tasks only (server-side filter, no client-side filtering needed); `POST /api/v1/tasks/{id}/complete` — rep-only, no body.
  - `GET /api/v1/goals` — rep sees own goals only; `GET /api/v1/goals/{id}/progress` — open to both roles; `POST /api/v1/goals/{id}/log-value` — rep-only, body `{value}` (for `SALES_VALUE` goals only, 409s on `VISIT_COUNT`/`TASK_COUNT`).
- **After login, reject non-rep accounts at the UI layer** — mirror image of the dashboard's reject-non-manager check. If `/auth/me` returns `role !== 'rep'`, show a clear error ("this app is for field reps") and don't persist the session.
- **Token storage**: same deliberate simplification as the dashboard — `refresh_token` in `@capacitor/preferences` (Capacitor's cross-platform key-value store, the mobile equivalent of `localStorage`), `access_token` in memory only. Using `@capacitor/preferences` instead of raw `localStorage` because this is a native app where `localStorage` inside the WebView is less durable/reliable across app updates than Capacitor's own storage plugin — this is the one place mobile and web diverge in the auth pattern.
- **The API client (`client.ts`) is functionally identical to the dashboard's** — same 401-refresh-retry-once logic, same `ApiError` class, same `erasableSyntaxOnly` field-assignment workaround if this project's `tsconfig` also enables that flag (check; don't assume, but don't be surprised either). The only difference is the token-read/write calls go through `@capacitor/preferences` instead of `localStorage`. Do not redesign this logic from scratch — port it.
- **Idempotency keys are generated client-side per queued/sent item** via `crypto.randomUUID()` (available natively in any modern WebView Capacitor targets, no extra dependency needed) — every ping and every check-in this app ever sends carries a freshly generated one. This task doesn't yet need offline-queue durability for these keys (that's Phase 8b) — for 8a, a key is generated and used immediately in the same request, not persisted for later replay.
- **Background location posts on a fixed timer, not on every GPS callback.** `@capacitor-community/background-geolocation`'s `addWatcher` callback can fire far more often than the 15-60s cadence `docs/design.md` specifies for ping posting — store only the LATEST location from the watcher callback (a ref, not state that triggers re-renders on every GPS tick), and a separate `setInterval` posts whatever the latest stored location is every `PING_POST_INTERVAL_MS` (30 seconds, the middle of design.md's 15-60s range, as a named constant). If no location has been received yet when the timer fires, skip that tick (don't post `null`/undefined coordinates).
- **`addWatcher`'s `backgroundMessage`/`backgroundTitle` options are required for background operation on Android** (the plugin's own docs: without them, updates are only guaranteed in the foreground) — always pass both, with a real, honest message ("Atlas Field Force is tracking your location for field visits"), not a placeholder. `requestPermissions: true` so the OS permission prompt fires automatically on first use rather than requiring a separate manual permission-request step.
- No test-file scope creep: same calibration as the dashboard — `npm run typecheck` + `npm run build` (+ the Gradle build) are the primary gates; a small number of targeted vitest tests only for genuinely new logic (the ping-poster's timer/latest-value behavior is the one piece worth it here, mirroring the dashboard's `usePolling` test).

